[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of RVQ-Alpha. Top: An RVQ Encoder discretizes gene expressions, followed by a progressive Pretrain-SFT-RL alignment with an LLM to enable multi-modal reasoning. Bottom: The unified model drives diverse downstream applications, spanning single-cell, population, and perturbation analyses.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic describing an RVQ-Alpha model for single-cell data and downstream biological tasks.\n\nPanel a shows the main workflow and model architecture. On the left, a “Data Encoding Pipeline” converts “scRNA-seq Data” through an “RVQ Encoder” into “Discrete Tokens,” then into “Multi-Task Instruction.” In the center, the “RVQ-Alpha Model” is shown as a circular training loop labeled “Pretrain & SFT & RL,” with schematic AI/model icons. On the right, an “MLLM Architecture” box connects “Diverse Single Cell Data” to two components labeled “LLM” and “RVQ,” which feed into “Capabilities” including “Understanding,” “Generation,” and “Reasoning.”\n\nPanels b-d show “Downstream tasks.” Panel b is labeled “Single-Cell Task” and includes cell type identification, top-K gene identification, gene expression generation, and batch effect correction. Panel c is labeled “Cell Population Task” and includes homogeneous cell type identification, heterogeneous cell type identification with cell type matching at instance level, and homogeneous phenotype identification. Panel d is labeled “Cell Perturbation Task” and includes gene expression change prediction and differential expressed genes prediction, using pre-perturbation cell population state, perturbation details such as drug name and concentration, target gene name, and outputs such as upregulated/downregulated changes or differential gene lists.\n\nBiological source objects shown include scRNA-seq data, diverse single-cell data, cell populations, cell types, gene expression vectors, genes, perturbation conditions, drug perturbations, target genes, and differential expression outputs. The figure depicts transformations from single-cell molecular data into discrete RVQ tokens and instruction-style inputs, then into multimodal language-model components for prediction, generation, reasoning, and downstream single-cell analysis tasks."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 2: Core dual-tokenization architecture. Text instructions are processed by a BPE tokenizer (top), while gene expression data passes through the RVQ tokenizer (bottom). Both token streams are fused at the LLM layer ( p ( t | h , c ) ), enabling bidirectional encoding and decoding: the BPE and RVQ detokenizers reconstruct the original modalities from the unified representation.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow figure for tokenizing and modeling single-cell data with LLM-style discrete tokens.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Two parallel input streams are shown on the left:\n  - **Meta data**, represented as small document-like panels.\n  - **Expression data**, represented as multiple single-cell study plots labeled with studies such as “Study 1,” “Study 2,” “Study 3,” and “Study-N.”\n- The metadata stream passes through a blue block labeled **“BPE Tokenize”**, producing a **Words Vocabulary** example with tokens such as “Nanocells,” “are,” “ultra-small,” “cells,” “~10nm,” and “diameter.”\n- The expression data stream passes through an orange block labeled **“RVQ Tokenize”**, producing a colored grid labeled **“Single Cell codebook.”**\n- Both streams are converted into vertical sequences labeled as discrete tokens. The lower caption reads **“Single Cell Discrete Tokens.”**\n- A central model block labeled **“LLMs”** receives token sequences and shows a next-token prediction style formula/interface, indicating language-model processing over mixed metadata and expression-derived tokens.\n- On the right, generated or decoded token sequences pass through:\n  - **“BPE Detokenize”** for metadata, producing text output: “Nanocells are ultra-small cells.”\n  - **“RVQ Detokenize”** for expression data, reconstructing study-like expression data panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell expression data from multiple studies.\n- Associated metadata text describing biological/cellular concepts.\n\nTransformations:\n- Text metadata is transformed into BPE tokens and later detokenized back into text.\n- Single-cell expression data is transformed into RVQ discrete tokens via a single-cell codebook and later detokenized back into expression data.\n\nModel interface:\n- A central LLM operates on the combined discrete token sequences from metadata and expression data.\n\nVisible finding/claim:\n- The figure illustrates a multimodal tokenization framework where both textual metadata and single-cell expression data are represented as discrete tokens that can be modeled by LLMs and decoded back into their original modalities."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 15, "caption": "Figure 3: Specialized vs. mixed RL training. Each panel compares task-specific (blue) and mixed multi-task (red) RLVR training. Specialized training consistently outperforms mixed training across all four tasks, with the gap widening after step 300. Homogeneous cell type shows the largest relative improvement (+192.5%), motivating the MOPD separate-then-unify protocol.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a four-panel scientific line plot comparing LLM judge accuracy over training steps for two model conditions: a blue solid “Specialized model” and a red dashed “Mixed model.” Each panel shows smoothed accuracy curves with scattered point observations, shaded blue regions under the specialized-model curve, gridlines, and annotated peak or notable values.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Cell Type (Homogeneous)”\n- Top right: “Tissue Type (Homogeneous)”\n- Bottom left: “Disease State (Homogeneous)”\n- Bottom right: “Cell Type (Heterogeneous)”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- X-axis in all panels: “Training Step”\n- Y-axis in all panels: “LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1)”\n- Accuracy is shown as percentages.\n- Legend at top center: blue solid line = Specialized model; red dashed line = Mixed model.\n\nBiological/source objects represented:\n- Biological metadata prediction or classification categories: cell type, tissue type, and disease state.\n- Homogeneous and heterogeneous dataset/task settings are compared.\n- No microscopy images, cells, tissues, genes, or molecular structures are directly shown; the figure is entirely quantitative performance curves.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The plot appears to track model performance during training.\n- It compares specialized training against mixed-model training across biological annotation tasks.\n- Smoothed trend lines are overlaid on raw scatter points.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- Specialized models generally improve with training and often outperform mixed models.\n- Cell Type (Homogeneous) shows strong improvement for the specialized model, reaching an annotated value around 0.840, while the mixed model remains much lower.\n- Tissue Type (Homogeneous) shows both models closer together early, but the specialized model reaches an annotated value around 0.640.\n- Disease State (Homogeneous) shows smaller gains, with specialized and mixed curves closer than in other panels.\n- Cell Type (Heterogeneous) shows the specialized model improving substantially over the mixed model, with an annotated specialized value around 0.383.\n- Each panel includes a small annotation box comparing specialized vs mixed performance, with visible relative gains such as +192.5%, +15.7%, +10.3%, and +83.3%."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 18, "caption": "RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow diagram for an evaluation or judging pipeline. It contains multiple labeled panels connected by arrows and dashed routing lines.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Left panel: “Input” with two columns labeled “answer” and “metadata,” illustrated with document and database icons.\n- Middle upper panel: “Rule & Safety Guard,” containing three components:\n  - “Hard Constraints”\n  - “Prompt Injection Defense”\n  - “Rule Penalties”\n- Right upper panel: “Dual-Judge Semantic Engine,” with:\n  - “Answer Judge,” shown as a gauge/meter\n  - “Conditional Switch”\n  - “Reasoning Judge,” with bar-chart subdimensions labeled “Independence,” “Robustness,” “Sufficiency,” “Granularity,” and “Consistency”\n- Lower dashed-box pipeline:\n  - “Template Matching,” including “Predict Label,” “Regex Filter,” and “Scanner”\n  - “Semantic Mapping Engine,” comparing “Predict Label” and “Ground Truth” with categories “Exact Match,” “Vague/Directional,” and “Unrelated”\n  - “Electronic Switch,” with “Threshold Slider” and “Trigger Condition Judge Score > Threshold”\n  - “Multi-Dimensional Audit Hub,” with sliders for “Independence (30%),” “Robustness (25%),” “Sufficiency (20%),” “Granularity (15%),” and “Consistency (10%),” followed by “De-Biasing Audit & Dimension Weighting” and “Reasoning Quality Score”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The diagram shows an input answer/metadata flow through rule and safety checks, then into a dual-judge semantic engine.\n- A lower branch performs template matching and semantic mapping against ground truth.\n- Conditional/electronic switches route scores into a weighted multi-dimensional audit module.\n\nFindings shown:\n- No experimental results are shown. The figure presents a system architecture for rule filtering, semantic judging, label matching, and weighted reasoning-quality scoring."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 29, "caption": "Figure 5: Per-dataset cell type accuracy across models. Bar-chart comparison of RVQ-Alpha (built on a Qwen3-4B backbone with the chosen ( Nc = 8, C = 32 ) RVQ tokenizer and GSPO reinforcement learning) against LangCell, Cell-o1, and C2S-Gemma2-2B on 8 evaluation datasets. Sampling budget: RVQ-Alpha uses pass@8 (8 stochastic decodings, best-of-8 selected by LLM judge); baselines use pass@1 (single-sample predictions from released checkpoints). This asymmetry favors RVQ-Alpha; see §5.2 for discussion. RVQ-Alpha wins on A013 (49.4%), D095 (80.9%), D096 (54.4%), and D102 (47.8%); Cell-o1 wins on D094 (37.4%); C2S-Gemma2-2B wins on D099 (64.3%), D101 (34.7%), and D103 (96.0%; see §7 for a training-data overlap caveat). LangCell is the weakest model on every dataset, peaking at 27.2% on D096. Baseline selection criteria are described in §4.6.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a grouped bar chart comparing model accuracy across datasets.\n\nVisible content:\n- Single panel bar plot.\n- X-axis label: `Datasets`.\n- Y-axis label: `Accuracy (%)`.\n- Dataset labels on x-axis: `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, `D103`.\n- Legend at top with four model names:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Bars are color-coded by model.\n- Red star markers appear above the highest-performing bar for each dataset.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No biological objects, microscopy images, cells, tissues, molecular structures, or experimental specimens are visually shown.\n- The figure appears to summarize biological or cell-related benchmark results only through dataset/model names, but the plot itself contains no visible biological source imagery.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No workflow diagram, model architecture, embeddings, attention maps, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n- The only model-related information is comparative performance by named models.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC-8_CS-32-GSPO` is highest on several datasets, including `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D101`, and `D102`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D099` and `D103`.\n- Approximate highest values shown include about `85.0` for `D095` with Qwen, `64.3` for `D099` with C2S-Gemma2-2B, and `96.8` for `D103` with C2S-Gemma2-2B.\n- Accuracy varies substantially across datasets and models, with some models near zero or low single digits on certain datasets."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 30, "caption": "Figure 6: Per-dataset Lowest Common Ancestor Distance (LCAD; Eq. 24) across models. Lower is better: LCAD measures the unweighted edge distance between predicted and ground-truth cell types along is-a relations in the Cell Ontology DAG [53, 10]. Sampling budgets are identical to Figure 5: RVQ-Alpha at pass@8, baselines at pass@1. RVQ-Alpha achieves the lowest mean LCAD on A013 (2.40) and D095 (2.22); C2S-Gemma2-2B leads on four datasets (D096, D099, D102, and notably D103 with LCAD = 0.39). LangCell's bar heights span 2.79-7.53 across datasets, consistent with the cross-lineage nature of its errors. See Table 15 for the per-dataset ontology-vs-fallback resolution breakdown.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a grouped bar chart comparing `LCAD Mean` across datasets labeled `A013`, `D094`, `D095`, `D096`, `D099`, `D101`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Datasets`\n- Y-axis: `LCAD Mean`\n- Legend/model labels:\n  - `Qwen3-4B-Thinking_NC=8_CS=32-GSPO`\n  - `LangCell`\n  - `Cell-O1`\n  - `C2S-Gemma2-2B`\n- Each dataset has four colored bars corresponding to the four methods/models.\n- Red star markers appear above some bars, indicating highlighted or selected values, but the figure does not define their meaning.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, model architecture diagrams, or transformation workflows are visible. The figure appears to report quantitative model performance or metric comparison across datasets.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- `LangCell` has the highest bars for several datasets, including `A013`, `D095`, `D096`, `D102`, and `D103`.\n- `Cell-O1` is highest on `D099`.\n- `C2S-Gemma2-2B` is highest on `D094` and `D101`.\n- The chart compares model-level `LCAD Mean` values rather than showing raw biological imagery."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 31, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel line chart showing LLM judge accuracy versus number of generations `k`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Number of Generations (k)` with values `1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16`.\n- Y-axis: `LLM Judge Accuracy`, scaled from about `60%` to `95%`.\n- Legend at top with three series:\n  - `Cell Type` in blue\n  - `Disease State` in red\n  - `Tissue Type` in teal\n\nFindings shown:\n- Accuracy increases as the number of generations increases for all three categories.\n- `Cell Type` starts around `84%` at `k=1` and reaches about `88%` at `k=16`.\n- `Disease State` starts around `68%` and rises to about `81%`.\n- `Tissue Type` starts around `79%` and reaches about `90%`.\n- At `k=16`, highlighted diamond markers indicate final values, with `Tissue Type` highest, `Cell Type` slightly lower, and `Disease State` lowest.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The plotted categories refer to biological/biomedical metadata labels: cell type, disease state, and tissue type.\n- No actual biological images, cells, tissue microscopy, or molecular structures are visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize an LLM judging or classification/evaluation process over multiple generated outputs.\n- No model architecture, interface diagram, or data transformation pipeline is shown."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 34, "caption": "RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic flow diagram showing a hierarchical biological classification framework from “All Cells” through five labeled concept-block stages:\n\n- **CB-0 Major Cell Group**: Immune (03), Epithelial (30), Stromal Mesenchymal (07), Endothelial (24), Neural Glial (25), Parenchymal Other (10).\n- **CB-1 Representative Subtype**: T/NK (00), B/Plasma (30), Myeloid (03), Basal (01), Secretory (22), Absorptive (09), Fibroblast (23), Pericyte/SMC (28), Myofibroblast (15), Capillary (04), Arterial/Venous (17), Lymphatic (05), Neuron (31), Astro/Oligo (14), Microglia/Schwann (16), Tissue-specific (26), Other (07).\n- **CB-2 Shared Cell State**: Proliferating (12), Quiescent (18), Activated Inflammatory (08), Stressed (11), Differentiating Transitional (09), Senescent Damage-associated (10).\n- **CB-3 Functional Program**: Cell Cycle (03), Proteostasis Translation (03), OXPHOS Metabolism (19), Immune Inflammatory Response (18), ECM Adhesion (28), Secretory Effector Program (08).\n- **CB-4 Pathway Activity**: IFN / NF-KB (03), TGFβ / EMT (14), WNT / Notch (19), mTOR / MYC (03), Hypoxia / P53 (27).\n\nColored vertical backgrounds separate the blocks: green for major cell group, blue for subtype, yellow for shared cell state, gray for functional program, and peach for pathway activity. Curved connector lines show mappings between categories across blocks. Black/gray lines connect cell groups to representative subtypes, blue lines connect subtypes and states to functional programs, and orange lines connect functional programs to pathway activities.\n\nThe biological source objects represented are cell populations, cell subtypes, cell states, functional transcriptional/metabolic programs, and pathway activities. The figure appears to summarize a cell annotation or concept-bottleneck style taxonomy linking cell identity to shared biological states, functional programs, and signaling/pathway activity categories."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 35, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single time-series line plot showing training dynamics over `Training Step` on the x-axis. The left y-axis is labeled `Response Length / Max (tokens)` in teal, and the right y-axis is labeled `LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1)` in red.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Teal curve: `Response Length (smoothed, w=25)` with faint noisy raw trace behind it.\n- Red curve: `LLM Judge Accuracy (smoothed, w=5)` with faint raw trace behind it.\n- Three shaded training phases:\n  - Blue region labeled `Initial`\n  - Orange region labeled `Converging`\n  - Green region labeled `Stable`\n- The response length rises from roughly 2,000 tokens to peaks near 9,000-10,000 tokens during convergence, then declines and stabilizes around 6,500-7,000 tokens.\n- The LLM judge accuracy starts near 0.15-0.2, fluctuates during early/converging phases, then increases and stabilizes around 0.7-0.8.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: smoothed training metrics are plotted over raw metric traces; the figure compares response length behavior with LLM judge accuracy during model training.\n\nFinding shown: accuracy improves and stabilizes while response length eventually decreases from an overlong peak and settles into a stable range."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 35, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with six line/scatter subplots arranged in a 2 x 3 grid.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Reasoning Quality Weighted Score (r=0.91)”\n- Top middle: “Independence (r=0.87)”\n- Top right: “Robustness (r=0.60)”\n- Bottom left: “Causal (r=0.84)”\n- Bottom middle: “Granularity (r=0.92)”\n- Bottom right: “Consistency (r=0.85)”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Each subplot uses “Training Step” on the x-axis, spanning roughly 0 to 800.\n- Left y-axis is labeled “Accuracy” in blue.\n- Right y-axis is labeled “Something Capacity” or similar in red; the exact wording is hard to read.\n- Blue scatter points and a smoothed blue trend line represent accuracy.\n- Red scatter points and a smoothed red trend line represent the second metric.\n- Faint dashed trend/reference lines appear in several panels.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. This is not a biological microscopy, anatomical, molecular, or specimen image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to visualize model training dynamics over training steps.\n- Metrics are plotted as scatter observations with smoothed fitted curves.\n- Correlation coefficients are reported in each panel title.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Accuracy generally increases with training steps across all panels.\n- The red metric also generally increases over training, though with different trajectories by metric.\n- Strong positive correlations are shown for reasoning quality weighted score, independence, causal, granularity, and consistency.\n- Robustness shows a weaker positive relationship, with r=0.60."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 41, "caption": "Figure 10: Semantic confusion matrix for homogeneous cell-type classification. 18 × 18 Coarse Ontology heatmap on the held-out evaluation set. The diagonal dominates (8,795 Enterocyte cells are correctly classified), and most off-diagonal mass concentrates between ontologically adjacent cell types (1,876 Enteroendocrine and Tuft cells are predicted as Goblet), demonstrating that RVQ tokenization preserves local semantic structure of gene expression.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel scientific visualization showing a confusion-matrix-like grid for cell type classification.\n\nVisible content:\n- Y-axis label: “Ground Truth Label”\n- X-axis label: “Semantically Corrected Prediction”\n- Row and column classes include: Astrocyte, Microglia, Oligodendrocyte, OPC, Excitatory neuron, Inhibitory neuron, Endothelial, B cell, Plasma cell, T/NK cell, Myeloid/Innate immune, Enterocyte, Goblet/Secretory epithelial, Paneth cell, Stem/Progenitor/Cycling epithelial, Enteroendocrine/Tuft, Platelet/RBC/CPC, and other.\n- Each cell contains a rounded square with a circular marker and a numeric count.\n- Strong diagonal counts indicate correct or semantically matched predictions for many classes, including examples such as Astrocyte 32, Microglia 30, OPC 32, Excitatory neuron 31, Inhibitory neuron 31, Enterocyte 65, Goblet/Secretory epithelial 61, Stem/Progenitor/Cycling epithelial 37, and other 0.\n- Off-diagonal entries show errors or semantic corrections, with several nonzero misclassifications, including around epithelial and immune categories.\n- Right side has two vertical color legends:\n  - Orange/yellow legend labeled “Correct Predictions,” ranging from 0 to 70.\n  - Purple legend labeled “Confusion Errors,” ranging from 0 to 20.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell-type labels from biological tissue/cell annotation, including neural, immune, blood, endothelial, and intestinal epithelial cell classes.\n\nModel/interface context:\n- The figure appears to evaluate a classification model or annotation system by comparing ground-truth cell labels against semantically corrected predicted labels.\n\nMain finding:\n- Most predictions lie on the diagonal, suggesting generally strong agreement after semantic correction, with notable residual confusion among related epithelial and immune/blood-associated categories."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 42, "caption": "Figure 11: RVQ embedding space by cell type. t-SNE visualization with perplexity 50 of LLM input embeddings for RVQ-encoded cells. For each cell, the 8 codebook embeddings from the model's expanded vocabulary are averaged to produce a single cell-level representation (26 cell types, N = 1,601 cells). Biologically related cell types cluster together: Neuron ( n = 366, upper right) forms a distinct cluster; immune subtypes (CD4+ T, n = 204; Myeloid, n = 201; T/NK, n = 117; CD8+ T, n = 83) overlap at center-left; epithelial types (Enterocyte, n = 58; Goblet, n = 18) form compact clusters. The separation of neuronal from immune and epithelial lineages demonstrates that RVQ tokenization preserves major biological axes in the LLM embedding space, complementing the classification-level evidence in Figure 10.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel scatter plot showing a t-SNE embedding of cell populations. The plot title is “Cell Types.” The x-axis is labeled “t-SNE Dimension 1” and the y-axis is labeled “t-SNE Dimension 2.” Points are colored by cell type, with a legend on the right listing categories and counts.\n\nVisible biological source objects are cell types, including B cell, CD4+ T cell, Unconventional T cell, Treg, CD8+ T cell, NK cell, Dendritic cell, Monocyte, Neuron, T cell, Endothelial cell, Macrophage, Fibroblast, Schwann cell, Kidney epithelial cell, Adrenal cortical cell, Myeloid cell, T/NK cell, Erythrocyte, Multiplet, Stem cell OLFM4+, Enterocyte, Goblet cell, Cycling cell, Stem cell, and Plasma cell.\n\nTransformation shown: high-dimensional single-cell data projected into two dimensions using t-SNE. No model architecture or interface is visible. The figure shows several separated and partially overlapping clusters, indicating cell-type-associated structure in the embedding."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 43, "caption": "RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a Sankey/alluvial flow diagram titled **“Cell Type Misclassification Flow (Errors Only, Raw Counts)”**.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left side label: **“True Cell Type”**\n- Right side label: **“Predicted Cell Type”**\n- Each left node represents a true immune/cell annotation, and each right node represents the predicted class.\n- Colored bands connect true labels to predicted labels, showing misclassification flows only.\n- Band thickness appears to encode raw error counts.\n\nVisible biological source objects / labels include immune and blood cell types such as:\n- γδ T cell\n- Transitional B cell\n- Regulatory T cell\n- Plasmacytoid dendritic cell\n- Plasmablast\n- Natural killer cell\n- Naive CD8 T cell\n- Naive CD4 T cell\n- Naive B cell\n- Mucosal-associated invariant T cell\n- Memory B cell\n- Hematopoietic precursor cell\n- Effector memory CD8 T cell\n- Effector memory CD4 T cell\n- Double negative thymocyte\n- Cytotoxic CD4 T cell\n- Conventional dendritic cell\n- Central memory CD8 T cell\n- Central memory CD4 T cell\n- CD16− CD56bright natural killer cell\n- CD14low CD16+ monocyte\n- CD14+ monocyte\n\nPredicted labels visible on the right include:\n- Dendritic cell\n- Conventional DC\n- Naive B cell\n- B cell\n- Naive CD8+ T cell\n- Naive CD4+ T cell\n- Effector CD8+ T cell\n- CD8+ T cell\n- Monocyte\n- Other\n- Alveolar macrophage\n- Classical monocyte\n- NK cell\n- Intermediate monocyte\n- Erythroid cell\n- Epithelial cell\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure visualizes classification errors from a cell type prediction model or annotation system.\n- True cell types are mapped to predicted cell types; correct classifications are excluded.\n- Misclassification counts are aggregated into flows.\n\nMain finding visible:\n- Several large error flows converge into broad predicted categories such as **Naive CD4+ T cell**, **Naive CD8+ T cell**, and **Other**.\n- A prominent misclassification flow originates from **Central memory CD4 T cell** toward **Naive CD4+ T cell**.\n- Monocyte-related classes also show confusion among predicted monocyte/macrophage categories."]}, {"figure_index": 14, "page_no": 44, "caption": "Figure 13: Semantic confusion matrix for homogeneous tissue-type classification. Companion to the cell-type confusion matrix in Figure 10. A 10 × 10 heatmap covering Ileum, Colon, PBMC, Entorhinal Cortex, Adrenal Gland, Lymph Node, Chest Wall, Neck, Liver, and Nose (the converged GSPO tissue policy; best-of-32 sampling; offline evaluation judge threshold 0.75, the tissue-population operating point of §4.5; external test sets D101, D102, and D103, defined in §4.5). Overall diagonal mass 13,833/16,651 ≈ 83.1%. Ileum dominates the diagonal (7,156, matrix maximum), followed by Colon (5,014). The largest off-diagonal cell is Colon → Ileum (1,294), with a smaller reciprocal Ileum → Colon (519), reflecting mutual confusion between gut tissues. Chest Wall, Neck, and Liver have diagonal zeros-Liver acts as a catch-all incorrect prediction for several rare tissues (receiving Ileum → Liver 361, Colon → Liver 242, PBMC → Liver ∼ 200), absorbing mass it never correctly predicts itself. The gut-tissue dominance and Liver sink pattern are consistent with the training data's tissue frequency distribution.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a confusion-matrix-style scientific figure comparing **ground truth labels** on the y-axis with **sequentially generated predictions** on the x-axis.\n\nVisible labels/classes include:\n`Ileum`, `Colon`, `Pbmc`, `Entorhinal Cortex`, `Adrenal Gland`, `Lymph Node`, `Liver`, `Chest Wall`, `Neck`, `Nose`, and `Other`.\n\nThe matrix cells show numeric counts, with strong diagonal entries such as:\n- Ileum: `798`\n- Colon: `615`\n- Pbmc: `152`\n- Entorhinal Cortex: `71`\n- Adrenal Gland: `36`\n- Lymph Node: `29`\n- Neck: `3`\n- Nose: `7`\n\nThere is a rightmost column summarizing **confusion errors**, shown with purple shading and values such as `91`, `52`, `9`, `15`, `41`, `14`, `21`, `21`, `17`, `0`, `0`.\n\nA vertical yellow color bar labeled **Correct Predictions** ranges from `0` to `800`, and a purple color bar labeled **Confusion Errors** ranges from `0` to `100`.\n\nBiological source objects appear to be tissue or sample-origin categories, including gastrointestinal tissue, immune cells, brain region, endocrine tissue, lymphatic tissue, liver, body sites, and an `Other` category.\n\nThe figure indicates that most correct predictions lie on the diagonal, while off-diagonal values are sparse, suggesting relatively strong class-wise prediction performance with remaining errors summarized in the rightmost column."]}, {"figure_index": 15, "page_no": 45, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a confusion-matrix style scientific figure showing classification results for disease/condition labels.\n\nVisible content:\n- A 6 x 6 matrix with rows labeled as ground truth and columns as predicted classes.\n- Y-axis label: “Ground Truth Label”.\n- Row and column classes:\n  - Crohn Disease\n  - Healthy\n  - Neuroblastoma\n  - Covid 19\n  - Dementia\n  - Other\n- Each cell contains a numeric count inside a small red-outlined circle.\n- Diagonal cells are highlighted in yellow/orange to indicate correct predictions.\n- Off-diagonal cells are shaded purple/blue to indicate confusion errors.\n- Two vertical color legends appear on the right:\n  - “Correct Predictions” with yellow scale from 0 to 240.\n  - “Confusion Errors” with purple scale from 0 to 1100.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Correct prediction counts on the diagonal include:\n  - Crohn Disease: 39\n  - Healthy: 234\n  - Neuroblastoma: 1\n  - Covid 19: 6\n  - Dementia: 53\n  - Other: 0\n- The largest visible confusion is Crohn Disease ground truth predicted as Other: 1030.\n- Other large confusions into “Other” include Healthy: 134, Neuroblastoma: 126, and Covid 19: 144.\n- No biological source objects such as cells, tissues, microscopy, organs, or molecular structures are shown directly.\n- The figure represents model evaluation output, not raw biological imagery."]}, {"figure_index": 16, "page_no": 52, "caption": "Figure 15: Per-cell-type accuracy distribution - cell type classification. Lollipop chart of percell-type accuracy on the homogeneous cell-type task (the converged GSPO cell-type policy; offline evaluation judge threshold 0.75; 9 lineage groups). High tier ( ≥ 0.8): B cell, Plasma cell, and Enterocyte BEST4+ all achieve 1.000, along with Stem cell OLFM4+ (0.991), Excitatory neuron (0.953), Enterocyte CA1+CA2+CA4 -(0.900), Paneth cell (0.856), and Goblet cell SPINK4+ (0.830). Medium tier : Microglia (0.777), Stem cell OLFM4+ LGR5+ (0.724), Goblet cell MUC2+ TFF1 -(0.700), Inhibitory neuron (0.660), Goblet cell MUC2+ TFF1+ (0.655). Low tier : Dendritic cell (0.020), Enteroendocrine cell (0.020), and three zero-accuracy types (Multiplet, Red blood cell, Tuft cell). The long lower tail reflects rare and ontologically proximate cell types that the model struggles to distinguish.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel quantitative plot showing mean accuracy by cell type/class, with horizontal dashed guide lines and colored performance bands.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: “Mean Accuracy”, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0.\n- Y-axis lists biological cell-type labels.\n- Legend indicates three accuracy groups:\n  - Low: red\n  - Medium: blue\n  - High: green\n- Background bands:\n  - Pink/red low-accuracy region at bottom\n  - Light blue medium-accuracy region in middle\n  - Light green high-accuracy region at top\n- Bubble markers at the right or near zero indicate each class’s mean accuracy; larger bubbles correspond to higher displayed values.\n\nBiological source objects / labels shown:\n- High group: B cell, Plasma cell, Enterocyte BEST4+, Stem cell OLFM4+, Excitatory neuron, Enterocyte CA1+ CA2+ CA4-, Paneth cell, Goblet cell SPINK4+\n- Medium group: Microglia, Stem cell OLFM4+ LGR5+, Goblet cell MUC2+ TFF1-, Inhibitory neuron, Goblet cell MUC2+ TFF1+\n- Low group: Dendritic cell, Enteroendocrine cell, Multiplet, Red blood cell, Tuft cell\n\nVisible findings:\n- Highest accuracies are 1.000 for B cell, Plasma cell, and Enterocyte BEST4+.\n- Other high accuracies include 0.991 for Stem cell OLFM4+, 0.953 for Excitatory neuron, 0.900 for Enterocyte CA1+ CA2+ CA4-, 0.856 for Paneth cell, and 0.830 for Goblet cell SPINK4+.\n- Medium accuracies range from 0.777 for Microglia to 0.655 for Goblet cell MUC2+ TFF1+.\n- Low accuracies include 0.020 for Dendritic cell and Enteroendocrine cell, and 0.000 for Multiplet, Red blood cell, and Tuft cell.\n\nNo visible microscopy image, biological specimen photo, model architecture, workflow diagram, or transformation pipeline is shown; this is a performance summary chart for biological cell-type classification or annotation."]}, {"figure_index": 17, "page_no": 53, "caption": "Figure 16: Per-cell-type accuracy distribution - tissue type classification. Lollipop chart of percell-type accuracy on the homogeneous tissue-type task (the converged GSPO tissue policy; offline evaluation judge threshold 0.75; 10 lineage groups including separate NK/ILC and Stromal lineages). High tier : only two cell types qualify-Enterocyte TMIGD1+ MEP1A+ (0.901) and Stem cell OLFM4+ GSTA1+ (0.811)-far fewer than the eight High-tier entries in Figure 15. Medium tier : Secretory cell (0.719), Excitatory neuron (0.697), Oligodendrocyte precursor (0.691), Epithelial cell METTL12+ (0.603), Innate lymphoid cell (0.508), CD8+ T cell (0.422), CD4+ T cell FOSB+ (0.406), CD8+ T cell KLRG1+ (0.405). Low tier : includes Macrophage Metallothionein+ (0.245), Fibroblast SFRP2+ SLPI+ (0.115), and three zero-accuracy types (Adrenal cortical cell, Schwann cell, Fibroblast). The contraction of the High tier from eight to two entries foreshadows the cross-task difficulty gradient.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel quantitative plot showing mean accuracy values for multiple biological cell-type labels.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: `Mean Accuracy`\n- Y-axis: cell-type or cell-state labels, including:\n  - `Enterocyte TMIGD1+ MEP1A+...`\n  - `Stem cell OLFM4+ GSTA1+`\n  - `Secretory cell`\n  - `Excitatory neuron`\n  - `Oligodendrocyte precursor...`\n  - `Epithelial cell METTL12+...`\n  - `Innate lymphoid cell`\n  - `CD8+ T cell`\n  - `CD4+ T cell FOSB+`\n  - `CD8+ T cell KLRG1+`\n  - `Intraepithelial...`\n  - `Regulatory T cell`\n  - `Enterocyte CA1+ CA2+ CA4-`\n  - `Macrophage Metallothionein+`\n  - `Fibroblast SFRP2+ SLPI+`\n  - `Adrenal cortical cell`\n  - `Schwann cell`\n  - `Fibroblast`\n\nVisual encoding:\n- Bubble/lollipop-style horizontal plot with dashed guide lines from the y-axis to circular markers.\n- Three colored performance regions:\n  - Green: high accuracy\n  - Blue: medium accuracy\n  - Red: low accuracy\n- Legend labels: `Low`, `Medium`, `High`\n- A small semicircular gauge-like legend at the top left marks thresholds or reference values `0.1`, `0.35`, `0.6`, and `0.9`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types and annotated cell states, including immune cells, epithelial/enterocyte subsets, fibroblasts, neural-related cells, and adrenal cortical cells.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Highest mean accuracies are for `Enterocyte TMIGD1+ MEP1A+...` at `0.901` and `Stem cell OLFM4+ GSTA1+` at `0.811`.\n- Medium-range examples include `Secretory cell` at `0.719`, `Excitatory neuron` at `0.697`, and `Oligodendrocyte precursor...` at `0.691`.\n- Low-range examples include `Intraepithelial...` at `0.371`, `Regulatory T cell` at `0.360`, and `Macrophage Metallothionein+` at `0.245`.\n- `Adrenal cortical cell`, `Schwann cell`, and `Fibroblast` are shown with mean accuracy `0.000`.\n\nNo model architecture, microscopy image, molecular transformation, or explicit computational workflow interface is visible in this extracted figure."]}, {"figure_index": 18, "page_no": 54, "caption": "Figure 17: Per-cell-type accuracy distribution - disease state classification. Lollipop chart of percell-type accuracy on the homogeneous disease-state task (the converged GSPO disease-state policy; offline evaluation judge threshold 0.85-stricter than the 0.75 used for cell and tissue type, reflecting more demanding evaluation for disease state). No cell type reaches the High tier ( ≥ 0.8), completing the difficulty gradient cell type (8 High) → tissue type (2 High) → disease state (0 High). Medium tier : only Inhibitory neuron (0.519) and Excitatory neuron (0.424). Low tier : Oligodendrocyte precursor (0.385), Pericyte RERGL+ NTRK2+ (0.292), Astrocyte (0.248), Fibroblast SFRP2+ SLPI+ (0.167), Stem cell OLFM4+ LGR5+ (0.101), Glial cell (0.051), Endothelial cell DARC+ (0.020), Platelet (0.006), plus eight zero-accuracy types (NK-like cell, B cell, Macrophage Metallothionein+, Monocyte CHI3L1+, Intraepithelial lymphocyte, CD8+ T cell KLRG1+, Regulatory T cell, and T cell). The absence of any High-tier entry and the prevalence of zero-accuracy immune cell types confirm that disease-state prediction is the hardest of the three homogeneous-population tasks.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a ranked dot/lollipop-style plot of mean accuracy by cell type or cell population. The x-axis is labeled “Mean Accuracy” and ranges from 0.0 to about 1.0. Cell-type labels are listed on the y-axis, with higher-ranked entries at the top.\n\nVisible groups/labels:\n- Top two rows are shaded light blue and labeled in blue: “Inhibitory neuron” and “Excitatory neuron.”\n- Remaining rows are shaded light pink and labeled mostly in red, including “Oligodendrocyte precursor...,” “Pericyte RERGL+ NTRK2+,” “Astrocyte,” “Fibroblast SFRP2+ SLPI+,” “Stem cell OLFM4+ LGR5+,” “Glial cell,” “Endothelial cell DARC+,” “Platelet,” “NK-like cell ID3+ ENTPD1+,” “B cell,” “Macrophage Metallothionein+,” “Monocyte CHI3L1+ CYP27A1+,” “Intraepithelial...,” “CD8+ T cell KLRG1+,” “Regulatory T cell,” and “T cell.”\n\nThe plotted mean accuracy values visible beside points are approximately:\n- Inhibitory neuron: 0.519\n- Excitatory neuron: 0.424\n- Oligodendrocyte precursor...: 0.385\n- Pericyte RERGL+ NTRK2+: 0.292\n- Astrocyte: 0.248\n- Fibroblast SFRP2+ SLPI+: 0.167\n- Stem cell OLFM4+ LGR5+: 0.101\n- Glial cell: 0.051\n- Endothelial cell DARC+: 0.020\n- Platelet: 0.006\n- Several immune/epithelial cell categories: 0.000\n\nA legend at the top indicates three categories: Low in red, Medium in blue, and High in green. There is also a small partial circular/pie-like inset at the top with labels 0.1, 0.35, 0.6, and 0.9, likely indicating marker size or score levels. The main finding shown is that neuronal categories have the highest mean accuracy, while many immune and epithelial cell types have near-zero mean accuracy."]}, {"figure_index": 19, "page_no": 55, "caption": "Figure 18: Tissue type - response length vs. accuracy. Companion to Figure 9a for homogeneous tissue-type classification (the converged GSPO tissue policy). Three training phases are annotated: Initial ( ∼ 0-150 steps), Converging ( ∼ 150-300), and Stable ( ∼ 300-770). Response length (teal, rolling window of 25 steps) starts ∼ 1,500 tokens, peaks at ∼ 7,700 around steps 180-200, and settles to ∼ 6,0006,200 in the Stable phase. LLM judge accuracy (coral, rolling window of 5 steps) starts ∼ 0.44, climbs to ∼ 0.62 near step 190, then hovers at 0.55-0.61 through the Stable phase with local peaks ∼ 0.61 near steps 430-440 and 570-620. Both curves exhibit a much smaller dynamic range than the cell-type figure (Figure 9a), suggesting that the tissue-type task reaches a training plateau earlier and with less dramatic length compression.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle-panel line chart titled “Training Dynamics: Response Length vs LLM Judge Accuracy.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Training Step,” ranging roughly 0 to 750.\n- Left Y-axis: “Response Length / Mean (tokens),” shown in teal.\n- Right Y-axis: “LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1),” shown in red/orange.\n- Legend: teal line = “Response Length (smoothed, w=25)”; red/orange line = “LLM Judge Accuracy (smoothed, w=5).”\n- Background is divided into shaded training phases labeled “Initial,” “Converging,” and “Stable.”\n- Faint unsmoothed traces appear behind the smoothed curves.\n\nFindings shown:\n- Response length starts low, rises sharply during early/converging training to about 7,000-8,000 tokens, then declines and stabilizes around 5,500-6,500 tokens.\n- LLM judge accuracy rises early from about 0.43 to around 0.55, then fluctuates mostly around 0.57-0.61 in later training.\n- The figure compares response verbosity dynamics with judged accuracy over training.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy panels, molecular structures, or model architecture/interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 20, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Figure 19: Disease state - response length vs. accuracy. Companion to Figure 9a for homogeneous disease-state classification (the converged GSPO disease-state policy). Three phases: Initial ( ∼ 0-120 steps), Converging ( ∼ 120-260), Stable ( ∼ 260-610). Response length (teal) starts ∼ 1,500-1,600 tokens, peaks at ∼ 7,800-7,900 around step 290, then fluctuates between 6,500-7,100 through the Stable phase. Accuracy (coral) occupies the narrowest range of the three tasks (0.28-0.42): it starts ∼ 0.30, rises to ∼ 0.35 by step 40, drops to a trough of ∼ 0.285-0.29 around step 240 in the Converging phase, then rebounds unevenly through Stable with oscillations ( ∼ 0.39-0.40 at step 420, dip to ∼ 0.36 at step 480, peak ∼ 0.41 at step 535), ending near ∼ 0.40. Disease state shows the weakest length-compression effect and the noisiest accuracy trajectory, consistent with it being the hardest task.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel line chart titled **“Training Dynamics: Response Length vs LLM Judge Accuracy”**.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: **Training Step**, ranging approximately 0 to 600.\n- Left Y-axis: **Response Length / Mean (tokens)**, colored teal, ranging roughly 1000 to 9000.\n- Right Y-axis: **LLM Judge Accuracy (mean@1)**, colored red, ranging roughly 0.28 to 0.42.\n- Legend:\n  - Teal line: **Response Length (smoothed, w=25)**\n  - Red line with markers: **LLM Judge Accuracy (smoothed, w=5)**\n- Background is divided into three shaded training phases:\n  - **Initial**\n  - **Converging**\n  - **Stable**\n- The teal response-length curve starts low, rises sharply during convergence, peaks near the middle, then stabilizes around 6500-7500 tokens.\n- The red accuracy curve fluctuates early, drops during part of convergence, then increases and stabilizes around 0.38-0.41 later in training.\n- Faint vertical/raw traces appear behind the smoothed curves, indicating noisy unsmoothed measurements.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy, transformations, or model architecture/interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 21, "page_no": 57, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with six subplots arranged in a 2 x 3 grid. Each panel shows scatter points and smoothed trend curves over training steps.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top left: “Reasoning Quality Weighted Score (r=+0.73)”\n- Top middle: “Independence (r=+0.73)”\n- Top right: “Robustness (r=+0.10)”\n- Bottom left: “Causal (r=+0.72)”\n- Bottom middle: “Granularity (r=+0.74)”\n- Bottom right: “Consistency (r=+0.71)”\n\nAxes and labels:\n- Shared x-axis label: “Training Step”\n- Left y-axis appears in blue and represents “Accuracy”\n- Right y-axis appears in red and represents the corresponding quality metric for each panel\n- Blue scatter points and blue smoothed curves show accuracy trends\n- Red scatter points and red smoothed curves show metric trends\n- A faint red dashed horizontal reference line is visible in several panels\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. This is not a biological microscopy, anatomy, molecular, or organism image.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to compare model training progression against evaluation metrics.\n- Smoothed curves are overlaid on raw scatter observations.\n- Correlation coefficients are reported in panel titles.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Accuracy generally increases during early training and then plateaus or slightly declines in later steps.\n- Reasoning Quality Weighted Score, Independence, Causal, Granularity, and Consistency show strong positive correlations with accuracy, with reported r values around +0.71 to +0.74.\n- Robustness shows little correlation with accuracy, reported as r=+0.10.\n- Several red metric curves rise sharply around mid-training steps, especially in the reasoning quality, independence, causal, granularity, and consistency panels."]}, {"figure_index": 22, "page_no": 58, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot arranged as a 2 x 3 grid. Each panel shows scatter points with smoothed trend lines over training progression.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Top left: “Reasoning Quality Weighted Score (r=0.70)”\n- Top middle: “Independence (r=0.38)”\n- Top right: “Robustness (r=-0.13)”\n- Bottom left: “Causal (r=0.36)”\n- Bottom middle: “Granularity (r=0.07)”\n- Bottom right: “Consistency (r=0.46)”\n\nAxes and encodings:\n- X-axis in all panels: “Training Step”\n- Left Y-axis: “Accuracy,” shown in blue\n- Right Y-axis: panel-specific metric, shown in red\n- Blue scatter points and solid blue smoothed lines represent accuracy trends\n- Red scatter points and dashed red smoothed lines represent the corresponding evaluation metric trend\n- Each panel includes light gridlines and dual Y-axes\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible. The figure does not show cells, tissues, molecules, organisms, microscopy, sequencing data, or other biological objects.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize model training/evaluation behavior across training steps.\n- No architecture diagram, model interface, input/output schema, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Accuracy generally increases across training steps in all panels, with an early dip around roughly 200-250 training steps.\n- Reasoning Quality Weighted Score shows a positive association with accuracy, reported as r=0.70.\n- Consistency also shows a positive association, r=0.46.\n- Independence and Causal show weaker positive associations, r=0.38 and r=0.36.\n- Robustness shows a slight negative association, r=-0.13.\n- Granularity shows near-zero association, r=0.07."]}]

