r_exclude,"The abstract details a multimodal predictive pipeline using pathomics and transcriptomics but lacks any natural‑language text component or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC2_no_text_component.","The abstract describes a multimodal pipeline integrating pathology images and transcriptomic data to predict mutations, with no mention of natural-language text or generative modeling, so it is an application wrapper rather than a primary text‑bio model.",The abstract describes mutation classification using multimodal ML models and provides no evidence of a generative architecture.,"The abstract details a multimodal predictive pipeline using pathomics and transcriptomics but lacks any natural‑language text component or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC2_no_text_component."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:146:980,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.","No text/language component; the study uses machine learning on clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for subtyping, without any natural-language text or text-bio alignment.","Abstract describes PD subtyping using ML/DL and drug repurposing; no generative model, text generation, or foundation model evidence present.","The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a machine learning analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping, with no mention of natural language text or generative modeling. Excluded because no text component and no generative model.","The paper applies machine learning and deep learning to clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for Parkinson's subtyping and drug repurposing; no generative model, natural-language text component, or text-bio bridge is described, so it is out of scope.","The abstract describes subtyping and progression analysis of Parkinson's disease using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of generative mechanisms, text generation, or biological sequence generation.","The abstract describes a machine learning analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping, with no mention of natural language text or generative modeling. Excluded because no text component and no generative model.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a PD subtyping study using multimodal data and ML/DL, with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model; thus it does not meet inclusion criteria.",No text/language component described; the study uses clinical progression data and multi-omics but no natural-language text or text-bio alignment.,"The abstract describes subtyping Parkinson's disease using machine learning and deep learning for phenotypic progression trajectories, with no mention of generative architectures, text generation, or biological sequence generation. No foundation-model characteristics are present.","The abstract describes a PD subtyping study using multimodal data and ML/DL, with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model; thus it does not meet inclusion criteria.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes multimodal clinical, imaging, and omics data analysis with machine learning but provides no mention of natural-language text, a text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism. Therefore it lacks a text component, bridge, and generative model, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.","The study applies machine‑learning/deep‑learning to multimodal clinical, imaging, genetic and transcriptomic data without introducing a new model. No natural‑language text, prompts, or documents are described, so there is no text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract focuses on PD subtyping and drug repurposing using machine learning and deep learning, without describing a generative architecture or foundation-model pretraining.","The abstract describes multimodal clinical, imaging, and omics data analysis with machine learning but provides no mention of natural-language text, a text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism. Therefore it lacks a text component, bridge, and generative model, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component. | The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.","The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component.","The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:149:945,Integration of multi-modal datasets to estimate human aging,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': 'Aging involves complex biological processes leading to the decline of living organisms. As population lifespan increases worldwide, the importance of identifying factors underlying healthy aging has become critical. Integration of multi-modal datasets is a powerful approach for the analysis of complex biological systems, with the potential to uncover novel aging biomarkers. In this study, we leveraged publicly available epigenomic, transcriptomic and telomere length data along with histological images from the Genotype-Tissue Expression project to build tissue-specific regression models for age prediction. Using data from two tissues, lung and ovary, we aimed to compare model performance across data modalities, as well as to assess the improvement resulting from integrating multiple data types. Our results demostrate that methylation outperformed the other data modalities, with a mean absolute error of 3.36 and 4.36 in the test sets for lung and ovary, respectively. These models achieved lower error rates when compared with established state-of-the-art tissue-agnostic methylation models, emphasizing the importance of a tissue-specific approach. Additionally, this work has shown how the application of Hierarchical Image Pyramid Transformers for feature extraction significantly enhances age modeling using histological images. Finally, we evaluated the benefits of integrating multiple data modalities into a single model. Combining methylation data with other data modalities only marginally improved performance likely due to the limited number of available samples. Combining gene expression with histological features yielded more accurate age predictions compared with the individual performance of these data types. Given these results, this study shows how machine learning applications can be extended to/in multi-modal aging research. Code used is available at https://github.com/zroger49/multi_modal_age_prediction .'}",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Integration of multi-modal datasets to estimate human aging,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': 'Aging involves complex biological processes leading to the decline of living organisms. As population lifespan increases worldwide, the importance of identifying factors underlying healthy aging has become critical. Integration of multi-modal datasets is a powerful approach for the analysis of complex biological systems, with the potential to uncover novel aging biomarkers. In this study, we leveraged publicly available epigenomic, transcriptomic and telomere length data along with histological images from the Genotype-Tissue Expression project to build tissue-specific regression models for age prediction. Using data from two tissues, lung and ovary, we aimed to compare model performance across data modalities, as well as to assess the improvement resulting from integrating multiple data types. Our results demostrate that methylation outperformed the other data modalities, with a mean absolute error of 3.36 and 4.36 in the test sets for lung and ovary, respectively. These models achieved lower error rates when compared with established state-of-the-art tissue-agnostic methylation models, emphasizing the importance of a tissue-specific approach. Additionally, this work has shown how the application of Hierarchical Image Pyramid Transformers for feature extraction significantly enhances age modeling using histological images. Finally, we evaluated the benefits of integrating multiple data modalities into a single model. Combining methylation data with other data modalities only marginally improved performance likely due to the limited number of available samples. Combining gene expression with histological features yielded more accurate age predictions compared with the individual performance of these data types. Given these resul