r feature extraction significantly enhances age modeling using histological images. Finally, we evaluated the benefits of integrating multiple data modalities into a single model. Combining methylation data with other data modalities only marginally improved performance likely due to the limited number of available samples. Combining gene expression with histological features yielded more accurate age predictions compared with the individual performance of these data types. Given these results, this study shows how machine learning applications can be extended to/in multi-modal aging research. Code used is available at https://github.com/zroger49/multi_modal_age_prediction .'}",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract reports regression models using epigenomic, transcriptomic, telomere, and histology image data with no mention of natural‑language text or generative modeling, thus it lacks a text component and a text‑bio bridge, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.","Abstract describes integration of epigenomic, transcriptomic, telomere length, and histology images for age prediction, with no mention of natural-language text or language modeling.","The abstract reports regression models for age prediction and uses a transformer for feature extraction, with no mention of a generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract reports regression models using epigenomic, transcriptomic, telomere, and histology image data with no mention of natural‑language text or generative modeling, thus it lacks a text component and a text‑bio bridge, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.",Integration of multi-modal datasets to estimate human aging,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': 'Aging involves complex biological processes leading to the decline of living organisms. As population lifespan increases worldwide, the importance of identifying factors underl